# Line endings are declared here so they stop being a rule anyone has to remember.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The repo was 1774 text files stored LF against 109 stored CRLF,
# and the CRLF side was eight hand-written sources plus the generated shader HLSL. Every
# editor and script in this project writes LF, so any scripted patch to one of those eight
# silently converted the whole file and buried the real change in a whole-file diff — it
# happened to geomwire.h (33-line change, 602-line diff), it happened to forgerender.cpp
# (which is why that file is LF today), and it happened again to renderprocess.cpp. A
# convention re-applied by hand on every edit fails eventually; a declared attribute does
# not, because git normalises on `add` and a working tree with the wrong endings simply
# reads as unmodified.
#
# It also removes the second half of the trap: with no CRLF text files left, an exact-match
# patch written with \n can no longer fail to match a file for reasons that look like a
# wrong pattern.
#
# text=auto means git decides text vs binary with its own NUL-byte heuristic, per file, so
# the .dxil / .nif / .dds / compiled-shader blobs are left byte-exact. The explicit binary
# lines below are belt and braces for formats we know, not a correction to it.

*               text=auto eol=lf

# Must stay CRLF: cmd.exe has label/goto edge cases with LF batch files.
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Must stay LF: run by bash under WSL.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Known binaries — pinned rather than left to detection.
*.dds           binary
*.nif           binary
*.dxil          binary
*.png           binary
*.ico           binary
*.ttf           binary
*.dll           binary
*.exe           binary
*.lib           binary
*.esp           binary
*.esm           binary
*.bsa           binary
*.resources     binary

# The generated shader tree is tool output: fsl.py writes CRLF on every build. Storing it
# LF is what stops those 101 files re-appearing in `git status` after each compile.
